# .env.proxy — the service definitions: every piece of the product, its host, port and prefix.
#
# Purpose   One origin. Every frontend and backend sits behind it, separated by prefix. This file
#           DEFINES each piece, it does not only route it: <PIECE>_PORT is the port the service
#           itself binds to (uvicorn --port, vite --port, next dev -p), and <PIECE>_PREFIX is the
#           path it mounts its routes under. The edge and the dev proxy then read the same keys
#           to route. One key, two readers, so a service and its route can never disagree.
# Readers   ctl (dev, ps), docker compose (build args, nginx envsubst), the nginx templates,
#           vite.config.ts / next.config.ts (dev proxy targets, base paths), backends that call
#           another backend (${ENGINE_URL} in config.yaml).
# Template  `ctl setup` copies this file to .env.proxy.
# Rules     Three keys per piece: <PIECE>_HOST, <PIECE>_PORT, <PIECE>_PREFIX. Backends add <PIECE>_URL.
#           A service reads its own PORT and PREFIX from here (config.yaml ${API_PORT}, ${API_PREFIX};
#           vite --port $WEB_APP_PORT). Never a port typed into code or a framework config.
#           HOST is localhost for `ctl dev`. Under docker, compose sets the service name as a literal
#           (api, engine, dashboard); this file is not consulted for that. `ctl up +env_override`
#           reads HOST/PORT from here instead, for a piece that runs outside this compose.
#           A prefix appears in the framework config as base/basePath through this file, never typed twice.
#           No secret here, ever. Everything in this file may end up in a frontend bundle.

# --- public origin (optional) ---
# Uncomment these three only for a real public deployment: the edge on the host's 80/443, and the
# origin the apps put into absolute URLs. `ctl up +public` reads them and refuses to start while one
# is blank. Local docker (`ctl up`, the +expose_web default) leaves them commented out: the edge is
# published on the _PORT of the piece that owns / (WEB_LANDING_PORT below; WEB_APP_PORT in the
# single shape), the same origin the dev server has, and nothing needs a public origin.
# PUBLIC_URL=https://<domain>          # what the browser sees. Never http://localhost: that is not public
# HTTP_PORT=80                         # published by ctl up +public as <HTTP_PORT>:8080
# HTTPS_PORT=443                       # <HTTPS_PORT>:8443; TLS certs must be mounted
DEV_PROXY_PORT=3080                    # docker/compose.dev.yaml — the one origin under ctl dev --proxy

# --- api: apps/example-api-python (FastAPI) ---
API_HOST=localhost                     # docker: api
API_PORT=8000
API_PREFIX=/api

# --- engine: apps/example-engine-rust (Axum) ---
ENGINE_HOST=localhost                  # docker: engine
ENGINE_PORT=8080
ENGINE_PREFIX=/engine
ENGINE_URL=http://${ENGINE_HOST}:${ENGINE_PORT}     # api → engine calls; docker: compose literal

# --- dashboard: apps/example-dashboard-nextjs (Next.js server) ---
DASHBOARD_HOST=localhost               # docker: dashboard
DASHBOARD_PORT=3000
DASHBOARD_PREFIX=/dashboard

# --- static frontends: apps/example-multi-web-app/<name> (built into the web image) ---
WEB_LANDING_PORT=3001                  # Next.js export. Owns / — exactly one piece owns the root, and it has no _PREFIX key.
                                       # ctl up +expose_web publishes the edge on this port (compose.m.expose_web.yaml)
WEB_APP_PORT=5173                      # Vite SPA. Also the port of apps/example-single-web-app-vite
WEB_APP_PREFIX=/app                    # single-frontend shape: /
WEB_DOCS_PORT=4321                     # Astro
WEB_DOCS_PREFIX=/docs

# --- external services (readers: backends) ---
# A service this product calls but does not run. URL only; its credential is in .env.secrets.
# PAYMENTS_URL=https://api.stripe.com

# --- compose ---
COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=<project>
